WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:03.819
Here's a sentence. The meeting was really
productive.

00:03.819 --> 00:07.765
You read it back, and something feels off.
Maybe it's the word.

00:07.765 --> 00:09.644
Maybe it's the whole sentence.

00:10.018 --> 00:14.512
Most editors give you exactly two moves.
Keep it, or delete it.

00:14.888 --> 00:17.494
Delete it, and your first try is gone for
good.

00:17.494 --> 00:20.488
Keep it, and the better version may never
get written.

00:20.488 --> 00:23.981
Either way, you have to decide before you've
finished thinking.

00:24.356 --> 00:26.930
Write-on gives you a third move. Keep both.

00:28.640 --> 00:33.115
Write-on began on November the tenth, the
day Anthropic released Fable 5.2.

00:33.115 --> 00:36.337
Timelines filled with people pushing it to
its limits.

00:36.337 --> 00:41.169
One question stuck instead: how do you write
more, and more thoughtfully, without

00:41.169 --> 00:43.377
handing your thinking to the machine?

00:43.751 --> 00:48.569
This is a short orientation, not a manual.
Write-on is meant to bend to the way you

00:48.569 --> 00:50.369
think, not the other way round.

00:50.744 --> 00:55.529
But four ideas make it click much faster,
especially if your hands already know

00:55.529 --> 00:56.983
another editor by heart.

00:58.690 --> 01:01.677
Idea one comes from Jason Fried's Write_On.

01:01.677 --> 01:04.802
Not version control, but alternative
control.

01:05.177 --> 01:10.461
Version control thinks in time: snapshots of
the whole document, Monday, Tuesday,

01:10.461 --> 01:14.440
Wednesday. To find an old phrasing, you dig
through the past.

01:14.815 --> 01:19.912
Alternative control thinks in place. The
other ways of saying this one line live

01:19.912 --> 01:21.186
right underneath it.

01:21.561 --> 01:25.492
Put the caret on a word and press
Ctrl+Shift+A.

01:25.492 --> 01:28.168
Now that word can hold variants.

01:28.751 --> 01:34.239
Hover and press the arrow keys, and you read
each option in context, in the sentence

01:34.239 --> 01:35.611
where it has to live.

01:35.986 --> 01:39.885
It works at three sizes, which Write-on
calls grains.

01:39.885 --> 01:44.446
A word gets a thin violet wave. A sentence,
a lower rose wave.

01:44.446 --> 01:47.389
A paragraph, a copper bar in the margin.

01:47.764 --> 01:50.674
And flipping between variants is not an
edit.

01:50.674 --> 01:53.584
Nothing is lost, and there's nothing to
undo.

01:55.290 --> 02:02.005
Idea two. Delete is a cliff. Write-on adds
two gentler slopes before it.

02:02.380 --> 02:08.279
The first is Ghost. Press Ctrl+/ and the
text fades almost to nothing, but stays

02:08.279 --> 02:09.828
exactly where it was.

02:10.411 --> 02:13.144
Now you can read the paragraph as if it were
gone.

02:13.144 --> 02:16.423
If it reads better, leave it ghosted. If
not, bring it back.

02:16.798 --> 02:21.986
The second is Overflow. A line you love, but
that doesn't belong here, goes to the

02:21.986 --> 02:25.593
side with Ctrl+Shift+X, and waits until you
want it back.

02:26.175 --> 02:29.971
So instead of keep or delete, you get a
dial.

02:29.971 --> 02:35.877
Keep it, hold a variant, ghost it, overflow
it, and only then, delete.

02:37.590 --> 02:42.636
Idea three is a small vocabulary. The
colours always mean the same thing.

02:42.636 --> 02:46.161
Green is kept. Amber is undecided. Red is
rejected.

02:46.535 --> 02:52.174
Every choice you make lands on a timeline,
which opens with Ctrl+Shift+L.

02:52.757 --> 02:58.404
Zoom out and you see the shape of a session,
bursts of drafting, stretches of

02:58.404 --> 03:02.217
cutting. Zoom in and each moment shows what
changed.

03:02.591 --> 03:07.163
You can replay a piece being written, then
click any moment and start editing from

03:07.163 --> 03:07.498
there.

03:09.210 --> 03:13.614
Idea four is the mental model. A draft isn't
one line of text.

03:13.614 --> 03:18.514
It's a tree of possibilities, and you decide
when each branch closes.

03:18.514 --> 03:20.219
Or whether it ever does.

03:20.594 --> 03:25.493
That gives you three habits. When you're
torn between two phrasings, don't choose

03:25.493 --> 03:26.642
yet. Add a variant.

03:27.017 --> 03:31.295
When you're unsure a line belongs, ghost it
instead of deleting it.

03:31.670 --> 03:34.823
And when you love a line that doesn't fit,
send it to Overflow.

03:35.198 --> 03:40.263
Your first minute: a welcome card and a
short tour walk you through each move once.

03:40.263 --> 03:43.863
Ctrl+Shift+R hides the tools when you want
just your words.

03:43.863 --> 03:47.707
The question-mark button brings the card,
and this video, back.

03:48.290 --> 03:53.485
Then write the way you always do. Reach for
these tools only at the moment you feel

03:53.485 --> 03:54.674
the urge to delete.

03:56.380 --> 04:01.532
Why does this matter? Because every flip,
ghost and stash is a small decision, and

04:01.532 --> 04:04.297
your decisions are a trace of how you think.

04:04.672 --> 04:09.271
If you pick the shorter variant eight times
out of ten, that's a pattern, and it

04:09.271 --> 04:10.133
belongs to you.

04:10.801 --> 04:15.962
To be clear about what exists: today,
Write-on is the workbench, plus one optional

04:15.962 --> 04:20.683
machine helper. Press Ctrl+Shift+G and three
suggestions arrive in blue, as

04:20.683 --> 04:24.459
alternatives you can flip to. They never
replace your words.

04:24.459 --> 04:29.558
Next, we plan to learn from your choices and
past writing, with quiet nudges that

04:29.558 --> 04:32.579
ask questions, and editor personas who
critique.

04:32.953 --> 04:36.512
One thing won't change: it never writes the
piece for you.

04:36.512 --> 04:40.869
It keeps every option within reach, and
learns to ask better questions.

04:42.579 --> 04:47.616
So the next time you reach for delete,
pause, and keep both.

04:47.616 --> 04:48.371
Write on.
